 "signs": [
  {
   "x": 28,
   "y": 21,
   "text": "THE ENTRANCE STONE. Kerbstone K1, the great stone at the door, carved with triple spirals and lozenges. Nobody knows what the spirals meant. People have been guessing for centuries and still can't pin it down."
  },
  {
   "x": 32,
   "y": 21,
   "text": "THE ROOF BOX. A small slot above the door lets the rising sun into the chamber for seventeen minutes around the winter solstice. A calendar, a church and a lightbulb in one, built five thousand years ago."
  },
  {
   "x": 11,
   "y": 26,
   "text": "BRÚ NA BÓINNE VISITOR CENTRE. Tickets, a cafe and a replica of the passage. Newgrange tours go by shuttle bus over the river. The winter solstice places go by lottery, and some years it rains anyway."
  },
  {
   "x": 12,
   "y": 24,
   "text": "BLUE PLAQUE: Newgrange was built about 5,200 years ago, older than Stonehenge and the pyramids at Giza.",
   "kind": "plaque",
   "dx": -0.475,
   "plaque": "newgrange_older_than_giza",
   "title": "Older Than Giza"
  },
  {
   "x": 26,
   "y": 20,
   "plaque": "newgrange_aengus",
   "title": "Aengus and the Dagda",
   "text": "In legend Newgrange, Brú na Bóinne, was the home of the Dagda, the great father god. His son Aengus Óg, god of love, asked to live there for a day and a night. Then he pointed out that all of time is made of days and nights, and kept it."
  },
  {
   "x": 9,
   "y": 25,
   "plaque": "newgrange_rediscovery",
   "title": "Found in 1699",
   "text": "By the 1600s the mound was a grassy hill. In 1699 workmen digging stone for a landowner, Charles Campbell, uncovered the entrance, and the Welsh scholar Edward Lhuyd came to see it and wrote it up. A lucky shovel, in the end."
  },
  {
   "x": 48,
   "y": 37,
   "plaque": "newgrange_dowth",
   "title": "Dowth",
   "text": "Dowth is the third great tomb of Brú na Bóinne, a little to the east. Its Irish name, Dubhadh, means darkness. Newgrange catches the winter sunrise, and one of Dowth's chambers catches the low winter sun as it sets. Two tombs, both ends of the shortest day."
  },
  {
   "x": 12,
   "y": 37,
   "plaque": "newgrange_hill_of_slane",
   "title": "The Hill of Slane",
   "text": "Just up the Boyne is the Hill of Slane. Legend says that St Patrick lit an Easter fire there in defiance of the High King at Tara, who had ordered every fire put out. The king was cross. Patrick was not put out."
  }
 ],
